course: dictionary
lesson: 10221
title: Small word, many jobs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word «to» is tiny, but it has lots of jobs. Most often it shows where something goes or who receives it, as in «give it to Sam». Read the words around «to» to see which job it is doing.
explain: «To» also comes before a verb, as in «I want to play» and «Alice has to run off to hide her laughter». Here «to» is part of the verb, so «to play» and «to hide» are actions. Ask yourself: is the word after «to» an action? Then this «to» comes before a verb.
explain: «To» can also show the end of a range, as in «from 5 to 9», and it is used in telling the time: «ten to six» means ten minutes before six. Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have the right job.
fact: «To» shows where something goes or who receives it, like «adds carbon dioxide to the air».
fact: When «to» comes before a verb, like «to startle», it is part of that verb.
fact: «Ten to six» is a time: it means ten minutes before six.
fact: «From 5 to 9» shows where a range stops, at the number 9.
fact: In quick speech and texts, «to» is often written as «2», and «gonna» means «going to»; these are informal, so do not use them in school writing.
exercise: «Burning coal, oil and gas adds carbon dioxide to the air.» What job does «to» do here? | It comes before a verb, like to run | It shows the end of a count | *It shows where the carbon dioxide goes | It is used in telling the time
exercise: In «Alice has to run off to hide her laughter», which word comes after the second «to»? | *hide | her | laughter | off
exercise: «It was ten to six.» What time is it? | Ten o'clock | Ten minutes after six | *Ten minutes before six | Six minutes before ten
test: In «The frilled lizard hisses to startle predators», what is the verb after the word «to»? | predators | hisses | *startle | frilled
test: «The shop is open from Monday to Friday.» What does «to» show here? | *The end of a range | Coming before a verb | Telling the time | Where something goes
exam: Which phrase is an exam-style use of «to»? | next to | to and fro | ten to six | *in order to
